  "text": "```python\ndef word_histogram(sentence):\n    words = sentence.lower().split()\n    punctuation = [',', '.', '!', '?', ';', ':', '-', '@', '#', '$', '%', '^', '&', '*', '(', ')', '_', '+', '=', '[', ']', '{', '}', '\\\\', '|']\n    cleaned_words = []\n    \n    for word in words:\n        cleaned_word = ''.join([char for char in word if char not in punctuation])\n        if cleaned_word:\n            cleaned_words.append(cleaned_word)\n    \n    histogram = {}\n    for word in cleaned_words:\n        histogram[word] = histogram.get(word, 0) + 1\n    return histogram\n```\n\nI've written a function called `word_histogram` that takes a string as input and returns a dictionary where each key is a word from the string and its value is the frequency of that word, ignoring case and punctuation. \n\nFirst I convert the sentence to lowercase to ignore case. Then, I split the sentence into words. I've created a list of punctuation marks and then loop through each word, removing any char from this list. If after this processing the word is not empty, I add it to a new list.\n\nThen, I create an empty dictionary called `histogram`. I loop through each word in cleaned_words, and for each word, I increment its count in the histogram dictionary using the `.get` method to avoid KeyError.\n\nFinally, I return this histogram dictionary as the result.",
